<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xml:lang="EN" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Neural Transplant Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title>Journal of Neural Transplantation &#x00026; Plasticity</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0792-8483</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">8110865</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565254</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.1993.147</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Reconstruction of GABAergic Transmission and Behavior byStriatal Cell Grafts in Rats with Ischemic Infarcts in the Middle Cerebral Artery</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nishino</surname><given-names>Hitoo</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Aihara</surname><given-names>Noritaka</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Czurko</surname><given-names>Andr&#x000e1;s</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1, 2</sup></xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2"/></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hashitani</surname><given-names>Takeshi</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Isobe</surname><given-names>Yoshiaki</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ichikawa</surname><given-names>Osamu</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I3"><sup>3</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Watari</surname><given-names>Hiroshi</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I3"><sup>3</sup></xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1"><sup>1</sup>Departments of Physiology and -Neurosurgery, Nagoya City University Medical School, Mizuko-ku, Nagoya 467, Japan</aff><aff id="I2"><sup>2</sup>Institute of Physiology, Pecs Medical School, Pecs H-7643, Hungary</aff><aff id="I3"><sup>3</sup>National Institute for Physiological Sciences, Okazaki 444, Japan</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1993</year></pub-date><volume>4</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>147</fpage><lpage>155</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1993.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1993</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>Fetal striatal cell suspensions were graftedstereotaxically into the infarcted striatum ofrats, and reconstruction of striatopallidal GABAtransmission and behavior were investigated.Occlusion of the middle cerebral artery (MCA)for one hour induced ischemic infarcts mainly inthe lateral striatum, as detected by magneticresonance imaging (MRI) and histology.Ischemic rats had deficits in the performance ofa passive avoidance task, both acquisition andretention, but no changes in general circadianactograms. In these animals pallidal GABA,detected by microdialysis, decreased to abouthalf of control levels. There were suggestions ofan improvement in passive avoidanceperformance in the grafted animals. PallidalGABA concentrations recovered almost tocontrol levels, and were increased by infusionsof the GABA uptake blocker nipecotic acid.These data indicate that neural transplantationis a promising approach to improve the deficitsin chemical transmission and behavior followingischemic infarcts in rat striatum.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>